# d6-03 — responsible use in practice
accountability|6.3|3|A team is asked who checked a particular Claude-generated figure before it was published, and nobody can say.|Establish a record of who checked what, since accountability needs to be traceable|Assume it was checked, since the team's process requires checking|Ask the team members whether any of them remembers checking it|Check the figure now and confirm that it was correct all along|Traceable accountability requires a record of who performed each check. Establishing that is what makes the question answerable in future.|Assuming the process was followed is what the question exposes. Recollection is unreliable, and confirming the figure now does not establish who checked it then.
data-sensitivity|6.2|3|An associate is asked to use Claude on data belonging to a different department that they have access to but no business need for.|Use only the data their own work requires, since access is not the same as need|Use it, since they have been granted access to the system it sits in|Use it and inform the other department afterwards|Use an anonymised extract of the other department's data instead|Access is provisioned broadly; the need-to-know principle is what governs use. Confining use to business need is the control.|Having access does not establish need. Informing afterwards follows the use, and anonymising does not create a business need.
confidentiality|6.2|3|A team wants to include real client examples in an internal training document produced with Claude.|Obtain permission or anonymise thoroughly enough that the client cannot be identified|Include them, since the document is only for internal use|Include them with the client's name replaced by a letter|Include them and mark the document as confidential|Client material used beyond its purpose needs permission or genuine anonymisation. Identifiability rather than naming is the test.|Internal circulation is still use beyond the engagement. Letter substitution rarely defeats identification, and a confidentiality marking does not authorise the use.
consequential-decisions|6.1|3|A team's process uses Claude to flag transactions as suspicious, and flagged customers have their accounts frozen automatically.|Separate the flag from the freeze so a person decides on the consequence|Improve the flagging accuracy so fewer legitimate customers are affected|Notify customers promptly when their account is frozen|Review frozen accounts within 24 hours so mistakes are corrected quickly|An automated flag driving an automatic consequential action removes the human decision. Separating detection from action restores it.|Better accuracy reduces frequency without restoring the decision. Notification and rapid review both follow a freeze that has already happened.
bias-fairness|6.4|3|A team wants to reduce bias in their workflow and proposes adding an instruction telling Claude to be fair.|Recognise that an instruction is not a control, and measure outcomes instead|Add the instruction, since it establishes the expectation clearly|Add the instruction and monitor for complaints|Add a stronger instruction specifying what fairness means in this context|Fairness is established by measuring outcomes, not by instructing the model. Recognising that instructions are not controls is the point.|Adding an instruction in any form leaves the outcome unmeasured. Complaint monitoring detects harm after it occurs.
disclosure-transparency|6.3|3|An associate is asked by a colleague whether a document they shared was written with Claude.|Answer honestly, since there is no reason to conceal it from a colleague|Say that it was a team effort|Ask why the colleague wants to know|Explain that the associate reviewed it thoroughly before sharing|A direct question deserves a direct answer, and evasion creates a problem where none existed.|A vague team-effort answer is misleading. Questioning the motive is evasive, and describing the review answers a different question.
acceptable-use|6.1|3|A team wants to use Claude to test whether their own systems can be tricked into revealing customer data.|Establish authorisation and scope in writing before any testing begins|Proceed, since the systems belong to the organisation|Proceed on a copy of the systems rather than the live ones|Proceed and report anything that is found to the security team|Security testing needs documented authorisation and scope even against your own systems. That is what distinguishes testing from an incident.|Ownership does not authorise an individual to test. A copy narrows the risk but not the authorisation question, and reporting follows unauthorised activity.
ip-and-attribution|6.3|3|A team is drafting a policy on whether generated content may be presented as the organisation's own work.|Decide it deliberately, since the answer affects contracts, marketing and client work|Allow it, since the organisation directed and paid for the work|Prohibit it, since generated content is not original work|Leave it to individual judgement on a case-by-case basis|Whether generated content may be presented as the organisation's own has contractual and reputational consequences. Deciding it deliberately is what makes practice consistent.|Both blanket positions assert an answer without weighing the consequences. Case-by-case judgement produces inconsistency in exactly the area needing consistency.
privacy-regulation|6.2|3|A team wants to keep a record of every Claude conversation for audit purposes, and conversations contain personal data.|Balance the audit need against retention limits and record the justification|Keep everything, since audit requirements take precedence|Keep everything but restrict access to the audit team|Keep summaries rather than full conversations|Competing obligations are resolved by weighing them and documenting the reasoning. That is what makes the retention decision defensible.|Asserting precedence skips the balance. Access restriction addresses a different risk, and summaries may be a good outcome but not the reasoning being tested.
accountability|6.3|3|A team wants to know what evidence they would need if a Claude-assisted decision were challenged in a year.|Decide now what to record, since the evidence has to exist before it is needed|Rely on the conversation history being available|Recreate the decision at that point using the same inputs|Keep a note of which decisions were made with assistance|Evidence for a future challenge has to be captured at the time. Deciding what to record now is what makes it available later.|Conversation history may not persist or may not capture inputs. Recreation produces a new result, and a bare note of tool use is not evidence about the decision.
data-sensitivity|6.2|3|A team discovers that sensitive data was pasted into Claude by a colleague who did not realise it was restricted.|Follow the incident process and address the training gap|Ask the colleague to delete the conversation|Note it informally and remind the team about data handling|Treat it as a minor issue since the data did not leave the organisation|A data handling breach follows the incident process, and the underlying cause is a knowledge gap worth addressing. Both parts matter.|Deletion does not undo the disclosure or record the incident. Informal handling bypasses the process, and minimising it skips both the process and the cause.
confidentiality|6.2|3|An associate wants to use Claude to summarise a document marked as restricted to a named group they belong to.|Confirm that using the tool keeps the material within the named group|Proceed, since the associate is a member of the named group|Proceed with the document's markings removed from the text|Proceed and avoid sharing the summary outside the group|Restriction to a named group constrains where the material may go, which includes systems. Confirming that is what keeps the restriction intact.|Membership authorises the person, not every destination. Removing markings conceals rather than complies, and controlling the summary does not address the input.
consequential-decisions|6.1|3|A team's Claude-assisted process affects individuals and has been running for a year without anyone reviewing its outcomes.|Review the outcomes now, since consequential processes need periodic assessment|Continue, since no problems have been reported during that time|Review the process design rather than its outcomes|Ask the people operating the process whether they have concerns|Processes affecting individuals need their actual outcomes examined periodically. A year without review is the gap the scenario identifies.|No reported problems is not evidence of good outcomes. Reviewing design does not reveal what happened, and operator perception is from inside the process.
bias-fairness|6.4|3|A team's fairness testing covers the groups they thought of, and someone points out a group they did not consider.|Extend the testing, since the omission is evidence the coverage was incomplete|Add that group and consider the testing complete|Explain that the original groups were chosen on a reasonable basis|Test the new group only if there is evidence of a problem affecting them|An omission surfacing this way indicates the coverage method missed things. Extending the testing addresses the method rather than the one instance.|Adding one group and stopping repeats the same partial approach. Defending the original selection ignores the finding, and requiring evidence first inverts the purpose of testing.
disclosure-transparency|6.3|3|A team's client asks for a description of the methods used on their engagement, and Claude was used throughout.|Describe its use accurately as part of the methods|Mention it only if the client raises AI specifically|Describe the methods without reference to the tools used|Say that standard industry tools were used in the engagement|A methods description that omits a tool used throughout is incomplete. Describing it accurately is what the client asked for.|Waiting for a specific question withholds part of the answer. Omitting tools and referring vaguely to industry standards both obscure the method.
acceptable-use|6.1|3|An associate is asked to use Claude to produce material that impersonates a named competitor's communications.|Decline, since material imitating another organisation's communications is deceptive|Proceed, since it is for internal competitive analysis only|Proceed if the material is clearly marked as a simulation|Proceed and destroy the material once the analysis is complete|Producing material that imitates another organisation's communications creates something that functions as an impersonation regardless of intent.|Internal purpose does not change what the material is. Marking changes the situation materially, and destroying it afterwards does not address its creation.
ip-and-attribution|6.3|3|A team wants to use Claude to translate a copyrighted work for internal distribution.|Check whether translation and internal distribution are permitted by the licence|Proceed, since translation creates a new work|Proceed, since the distribution is internal|Proceed and credit the original author in the translation|Translation is a restricted act and internal distribution is still distribution. The licence determines whether either is permitted.|The new-work argument misstates how translation rights work. Internal scope and attribution both assume permissions that have not been checked.
privacy-regulation|6.2|3|A team wants to use Claude on data about children, and the organisation has no specific policy on it.|Establish the additional protections that apply, since children's data is treated differently|Apply the organisation's general data handling policy|Anonymise the data and then proceed|Ask the parents for consent before processing the data|Children's data attracts heightened protection in most regimes. Establishing what applies is the prerequisite to any handling decision.|A general policy does not capture the heightened requirements. Anonymisation and parental consent are possible controls but presume the requirements are known.
accountability|6.3|3|A team's process is described in documentation that says a person reviews every output, and in practice reviews are skipped when busy.|Align the documentation with what actually happens, or make the practice match|Update the documentation to describe the review as a target|Ask the team to follow the documented process|Accept the gap, since the documentation describes the intended process|Documentation asserting a control that does not operate creates false assurance. Closing the gap in either direction is what restores honesty.|Softening the documentation without deciding the control is a partial answer. Instructing compliance without resourcing repeats the failure, and accepting the gap leaves the false assurance.
data-sensitivity|6.2|3|A team is deciding whether to allow Claude use on a category of data and has no way to monitor what people actually paste.|Combine clear guidance with technical limits rather than relying on guidance alone|Publish clear guidance and rely on people to follow it|Prohibit all use of Claude for that category of work|Allow it and address problems if they arise|Guidance alone does not constrain behaviour when nothing checks it. Pairing it with technical limits is what makes the control real.|Guidance without enforcement is the gap described. A blanket prohibition forgoes legitimate use, and reactive handling waits for a breach.
confidentiality|6.2|3|An associate realises a Claude conversation they had last week contained material they were not authorised to share.|Report it through the incident process rather than handling it privately|Delete the conversation and move on|Mention it to their manager informally|Note it for their own records in case it becomes relevant later|An unauthorised disclosure is an incident, and reporting it is what allows the organisation to assess and respond appropriately.|Deletion does not undo the disclosure or trigger assessment. Informal mention and private notes both bypass the process.
consequential-decisions|6.1|3|A team wants to use Claude to prioritise which safeguarding referrals are reviewed first.|Ensure no referral can be deprioritised beyond a defined time regardless of ranking|Improve the prioritisation prompt so serious cases rank higher|Have a person review the ranking each day|Use the ranking only as a suggestion that reviewers may override|Safeguarding referrals need a guaranteed floor on how long any case waits, because a ranking error there carries serious consequences.|Prompt improvement and daily review both leave the worst case dependent on the ranking. Advisory framing helps but does not bound the wait.
bias-fairness|6.4|3|A team's workflow has been tested for bias once, at launch, eighteen months ago.|Test periodically, since inputs, usage and models all change over time|Rely on the launch test, since the workflow has not been changed|Test again only if a complaint is received|Test again when the workflow is next modified|Fairness is a property of a system in its current context, and that context drifts. Periodic testing is what tracks it.|An unchanged workflow can still drift as inputs and models change. Complaint-triggered and modification-triggered testing both leave long gaps.
disclosure-transparency|6.3|3|A team is writing a public statement about their use of AI and wants it to be reassuring.|Make it accurate first, since a reassuring statement that is untrue is worse than none|Emphasise the human oversight in the process|Keep it general so it does not constrain future practice|Describe the benefits to customers of the approach being taken|A public statement about practice has to be true, because an inaccurate reassurance becomes a liability when the practice is examined.|Emphasising oversight, staying general and describing benefits are all presentational choices that do not address accuracy.
acceptable-use|6.1|3|An associate wants to use Claude to generate a large number of applications to a scheme with limited places.|Consider whether volume submission undermines the scheme and breaches its terms|Proceed, since each application will be genuine and accurate|Proceed but submit them under different names|Proceed and withdraw any that are not needed|Mass submission to a scheme with limited places usually breaches its terms and disadvantages other applicants. That is the consideration.|Individual accuracy does not address the volume. Different names compounds the problem, and withdrawal follows the distortion.
ip-and-attribution|6.3|3|A team wants to know whether Claude's output can be used in a product they will sell.|Check the terms for their plan, since commercial use rights are set there|Assume it can, since they generated the output themselves|Assume it cannot without a specific commercial licence|Ask a lawyer to advise on the position before proceeding|The terms of the plan govern commercial use rights. Reading them answers the question directly and authoritatively.|Both assumptions guess at a documented position. Legal advice is reasonable but starts with the terms.
privacy-regulation|6.2|3|A team is asked how they would delete an individual's data from their Claude-assisted process if requested.|Work out now where personal data ends up in the process|Say the data is not stored by the process|Delete the conversations containing the individual's data|Explain that deletion is handled by the tool provider|Answering a deletion request requires knowing where personal data resides across the process. Mapping that is the prerequisite.|Asserting nothing is stored may be wrong. Deleting conversations addresses one location, and deferring to the provider does not discharge the obligation.
accountability|6.3|3|A team's manager asks to be shown that the Claude-assisted process is producing correct results.|Show the verification evidence, since the manager is asking for assurance rather than description|Describe how the process works|Show them examples of good output|Explain that the team reviews the output before it is used|An assurance request is answered with evidence that checking happens and what it found. That is different from describing the process.|A process description, selected examples and a claim about review all describe intent rather than demonstrate results.
data-sensitivity|6.2|3|An associate is unsure whether a document contains personal data because it uses internal reference numbers rather than names.|Treat reference numbers that can be linked to individuals as personal data|Treat it as non-personal since no names appear|Treat it as personal only if the linking table is also present|Ask whether anyone could reasonably work out who the numbers refer to|Identifiers that can be linked to individuals are personal data even when the link sits elsewhere. Recognising pseudonymisation as distinct from anonymisation is the point.|Absence of names is not the test. Requiring the linking table alongside misunderstands the rule, and the reasonableness question is closer but the linkability principle is what governs.
confidentiality|6.2|3|A team wants to build a Project holding material from several clients to answer general questions about their sector.|Do not combine client material, since each client's obligations apply to their own|Combine it with the client names removed|Combine it and restrict access to senior staff|Ask each client whether they mind their material being combined|Each client's confidentiality obligation applies to their material regardless of what else is combined with it. Keeping them separate is the control.|Name removal does not release the obligation. Access restriction is a different control, and seeking permission is worth considering but the default is separation.
consequential-decisions|6.1|3|A team wants to use Claude to decide which customers are offered a hardship arrangement.|Keep the decision with a person, since it affects vulnerable people directly|Automate it using the documented hardship criteria|Automate the offers and route refusals to a person|Automate it and review the decisions weekly|Decisions about people in financial hardship affect vulnerable individuals and need an accountable person rather than an automated determination.|Documented criteria do not remove the need for accountability. Asymmetric routing still auto-decides, and weekly review follows the decision.
bias-fairness|6.4|3|A team's Claude workflow performs equally well across groups on average but has much higher variance for one group.|Treat the variance as a fairness concern in its own right|Accept it, since the average performance is equal across the groups|Investigate whether the variance is caused by smaller sample sizes|Add more examples from that group to the prompt|Equal averages with unequal variance means individuals in that group face a less predictable outcome. That is a fairness issue the average conceals.|Equal averages is the misconception being tested. Sample size is one possible explanation, and adding examples is a remedy chosen before the concern is recognised.
disclosure-transparency|6.3|3|An associate is asked to remove a reference to AI assistance from a document before it goes to a client.|Ask why, since the reason determines whether removing it would mislead|Remove it, since the person asking is more senior|Refuse to remove the reference|Remove it and keep a record that the reference was there originally|Whether removal is appropriate depends on why it is being asked. Establishing that is what distinguishes a formatting preference from concealment.|Complying on seniority and refusing outright both decide without the reason. A private record does not address whether the client is misled.
acceptable-use|6.1|3|A team is unsure whether a proposed use is permitted and the policy does not clearly address it.|Ask whoever owns the policy, since ambiguity is resolved by the owner|Proceed, since the policy does not prohibit it|Do not proceed, since the policy does not permit it|Proceed and document the reasoning in case it is questioned later|Ambiguity in a policy is resolved by the person who owns it. Asking produces an answer and usually improves the policy.|Reading silence as permission or prohibition both substitute a guess. Documented reasoning protects the individual without resolving the question.
ip-and-attribution|6.3|3|A team wants to use Claude to summarise articles from a news service and circulate the summaries internally.|Check the service's terms, since summarising and circulating are usually addressed|Proceed, since summaries are not reproductions|Proceed, since circulation is internal|Proceed and include a link to the original article with each summary|News service terms typically address both summarisation and internal circulation. Reading them is what establishes whether this is permitted.|The summary argument assumes a conclusion the terms may contradict. Internal scope and linking are both assumptions about permissions not yet checked.
privacy-regulation|6.2|3|A team wants to profile customers using Claude and is unsure whether individuals have a right to object.|Check what rights apply to profiling in their jurisdiction before starting|Assume no right to object since the profiling is for service improvement|Assume a right to object and build an opt-out|Ask customers whether they would want to opt out of profiling|Profiling often attracts specific rights including objection. Establishing what applies is the prerequisite to designing the process.|Assuming no right guesses at the law. Building an opt-out may be right but presumes the requirement, and asking customers is not the legal position.
accountability|6.3|3|A team is asked whether they can explain a particular Claude-assisted outcome to the person it affected.|Test whether they can, since being able to explain it is part of using it responsibly|Explain that the outcome followed the documented process|Provide the person with the inputs that were used|Explain that the outcome was produced with automated assistance|Whether an outcome can actually be explained to the person affected is a testable property. Checking it is what reveals whether the process is defensible.|A process reference, raw inputs and a statement about automation are all substitutes for an explanation of this particular outcome.
bias-fairness|6.4|3|A team is asked to show that their Claude-assisted process does not disadvantage any group, and they have never collected outcome data by group.|Start collecting outcome data by group, since the question cannot be answered without it|Explain that the process does not use any group characteristics as inputs|Point to the fairness instruction included in the configuration|Ask the people operating the process whether they have observed any disparity|A claim about disparate impact can only be supported by outcome data broken down by group. Collecting it is what makes the question answerable at all.|Not using group characteristics does not prevent proxy effects. An instruction is not evidence, and operator observation is neither systematic nor independent.
